<section class="phero"><div class="phero-media"><img src="/assets/img/photos/consult-table.webp" srcset="/assets/img/photos/consult-table-640.webp 640w, /assets/img/photos/consult-table.webp 960w" sizes="100vw" width="960" height="640" alt="" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"></div><div class="wrap phero-in"><nav class="crumbs" aria-label="Breadcrumb"><a href="/">Home</a><i aria-hidden="true">›</i><a href="/nh-appraisal-guides/">Guides</a><i aria-hidden="true">›</i><span aria-current="page">Appraisal Clause, Lawyer or Public Adjuster: Sorting Out Who Fits Your NH Claim</span></nav><p class="kicker">Guide · 7 min read · October 21, 2026</p><h1>Appraisal Clause, Lawyer or Public Adjuster: Sorting Out Who Fits Your NH Claim</h1><p class="lede">Appraisers, public adjusters and attorneys solve different problems. Matching the help to the kind of dispute you have saves time, money and frustration.</p></div></section>
<div class="wrap layout"><article class="prose">
<p>When a property claim stalls, people often reach for the first professional they hear about. But an appraiser, a public adjuster and an attorney do different jobs, and the right choice depends on what you and the insurer actually disagree about. Here is a practical way to sort it out.</p>
<h2>Start by naming the disagreement</h2>
<p>Most stalled claims fall into one of three buckets:</p>
<ol>
<li><strong>Price and scope.</strong> The insurer accepts the loss but its estimate is too low: missing items, thin pricing, a roof slope left out.</li>
<li><strong>Coverage.</strong> The insurer says the damage isn’t covered, or only part of it is — wear and tear, a vacancy condition, flood rather than wind.</li>
<li><strong>Conduct.</strong> Months of silence, shifting explanations, or an offer that seems disconnected from the facts.</li>
</ol>
<p>Many disputes mix all three. The trick is figuring out which one is driving the outcome. Read the insurer’s letters closely: phrases like “not covered,” “excluded” or “pre-existing” point to a coverage dispute, while a payment with a lower estimate attached points to an argument about amount. If you can’t tell, write a short summary of where you stand in your own words — what was damaged, what the insurer paid or refused, and why it said so. That one page will make any first conversation with a professional faster and cheaper, whoever you end up calling.</p>
<h2>What the appraisal clause is built for</h2>
<p>The <strong>appraisal clause</strong> in most New Hampshire homeowners and commercial policies is designed for the first bucket. Either side demands appraisal in writing, each picks an appraiser, the two pick an umpire, and any two can sign an award setting the amount of loss. It tends to be faster and cheaper than litigation, and it keeps the argument focused on measurements, pricing and scope.</p>
<p>What it doesn’t do is decide whether the loss is covered or whether the insurer handled the claim properly. If those are your real problems, an award may not help much.</p>
<h2>Where a public adjuster fits</h2>
<p>A public adjuster works for the policyholder to prepare and present the whole claim, often from the start. That can be useful when the loss is large and complicated — a fire at a Lakes Region lake house, a commercial building with business interruption — and you don’t have the time or knowledge to assemble it yourself. Public adjusters in New Hampshire are licensed through the Insurance Department, and their fees are typically a percentage of the recovery. Ask about the fee, the contract terms and how to cancel before you sign.</p>
<h2>When it’s time for an attorney</h2>
<p>Talk to a New Hampshire attorney when:</p>
<ul>
<li>The insurer has denied the claim in full, or denied a major part on coverage grounds.</li>
<li>You’ve been asked to sit for an examination under oath or to sign a broad release.</li>
<li>The insurer is alleging misrepresentation or fraud.</li>
<li>A suit-limitation date in your policy is getting close.</li>
<li>You believe the insurer’s conduct went beyond a reasonable difference of opinion.</li>
</ul>
<p>RSA chapter 417 lists unfair claim settlement practices, and the Insurance Department accepts complaints. New Hampshire doesn’t have a broad first-party bad-faith penalty statute; remedies depend on the policy, contract law and the facts — ask an attorney.</p>
<h2>A side-by-side comparison</h2>
<div class="table-wrap"><table>
<thead><tr><th></th><th>Appraiser</th><th>Public adjuster</th><th>Attorney</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>Best for</td><td>Disputes over amount of loss</td><td>Building and presenting a complex claim</td><td>Coverage denials, legal rights, litigation</td></tr>
<tr><td>Decides coverage?</td><td>No</td><td>No — argues for it</td><td>Advises; a court decides</td></tr>
<tr><td>Typical fee basis</td><td>Hourly, flat or percentage, by agreement</td><td>Percentage of recovery, by contract</td><td>Hourly or contingency, by agreement</td></tr>
<tr><td>Gives legal advice?</td><td>No</td><td>No</td><td>Yes</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table></div>
<h2>Combining them, and a few cautions</h2>
<p>These roles aren’t mutually exclusive. An attorney may handle a coverage fight and then recommend appraisal once coverage is resolved. A public adjuster may prepare a claim and later invoke appraisal on the amount. What matters is that everyone knows who is doing what and that fees don’t stack in ways that eat the recovery. Our <a href="/nh-claim-calculators/gap-and-fee-estimator/">gap and fee estimator</a> helps you test the math.</p>
<p>New Hampshire Insurance Appraiser handles appraisal-clause representation, estimate reviews and umpire work. We aren’t a law firm or a public adjuster, and we’ll say so when your problem calls for one. See our <a href="/nh-appraisal-services/appraisal-clause-representation-nh/">appraisal clause representation</a> page or check your file against the <a href="/nh-claim-calculators/nh-appraisal-readiness-score/">appraisal readiness score</a>.</p>
